All tests passed. Here's what I implemented in `list_ops.py`:

- **`append(list1, list2)`** — iterates through both lists, adding all items from the second to the end of the first
- **`concat(lists)`** — flattens a series of lists into one
- **`filter(function, list)`** — returns items where `function(item)` is True
- **`length(list)`** — counts items by iterating and incrementing a counter
- ****`map(function, list)`** — applies `function(item)` to each item, returning the resulting list
- **`foldl(function, list, initial)`** — folds from the left, passing accumulator and current item to the function
- **`foldr(function, list, initial)`** — folds from the right (reverses the list first, then folds left)
- **`reverse(list)`** — inserts each item at position 0 to build a reversed list

All implementations avoid built-in functions like `len()`, `sum()`, `reversed()`, `list.extend()`, etc., following the exercise's constraint.
